he three lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) are distinct. Exactly one of them (\\(L_{3}\\)) is sunny, while \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are non‑sunny.\n\nFinally we verify that every point in (1) lies on at least one of the three lines:\n\n| Point | Containing line(s) |\n|-------|--------------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) |\n\nThus all required points are covered, and the configuration contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L_{1}:x=1,\\;L_{2}:y=1,\\;L_{3}:y=x}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
