            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *\\(a\\)-th column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a ,\\qquad |S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk=0\\text{ is always attainable.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a\\qquad(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\n(all non‑sunny) and add a sunny line that passes through the unique point of the last column, namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nOne convenient choice is  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-(2n-1),\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S\\) is sunny and contains \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe vertical lines cover every point of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\); the sunny line covers the remaining point \\((n,1)\\). All \\(n\\) lines are distinct and exactly one of them is sunny. Hence\n\n\\[\nk=1\\text{ is also attainable for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least two sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nN=n-V=\\#\\{\\text{non‑vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1.  \\(V\\le \\dfrac n2\\).\n\nEach line meets a column in at most one point, except a vertical line which meets exactly one column (its own) in all its points. Counting the incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the \\(n\\) columns we obtain\n\n\\[\nV+N\\cdot n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(N=n-V\\),\n\n\\[\nV+n(n-V)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow (n-1)V\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow V\\le\\frac n2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(N=n-V\\ge\\frac n2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.  Structure of the columns without a vertical line.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_0=\\min\\{a\\mid C_a\\text{ contains no vertical line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a non‑vertical line can intersect a given column in **at most one** point, covering the whole column \\(C_{a_0}\\) needs at least \\(|C_{a_0}|\\) distinct non‑vertical lines; therefore\n\n\\[\n|C_{a_0}|=n+1-a_0\\le N=n-V .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInequality (4) is equivalent to \\(a_0\\ge V+1\\). Hence the first \\(V\\) columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_V\\) must each contain a vertical line, and there are exactly \\(V\\) vertical lines, so **no other column can contain a vertical line**. Thus the columns lacking a vertical line are precisely\n\n\\[\nC_{V+1},C_{V+2},\\dots ,C_n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor the smallest such column we have, using (4) with equality,\n\n\\[\n|C_{V+1}|=n-V=N .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEquation (6) shows that every non‑vertical line meets the column \\(C_{V+1}\\) in a **different** point; otherwise some point of \\(C_{V+1}\\) would remain uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 3.  Two sunny lines cannot coexist.\n\nLet the two sunny lines be \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\). By Lemma 2 they intersect the column \\(C_{V+1}\\) at two distinct points\n\n\\[\nP_1=(V+1,y_1),\\qquad P_2=(V+1,y_2),\\qquad y_1\\neq y_2 .\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie on the same anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=V+1+y_1 .\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two distinct points of \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); such a line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**. Consequently each of the sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) contains **no other** point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D\\).\n\nThe anti‑diagonal \\(D\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(x,\\,V+1+y_1-x)\\mid x=1,2,\\dots ,V+1\\},\n\\]\n\nhence it has exactly \\(V+1\\) points. Apart from \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) there are \\(V-1\\) further points of \\(D\\).\n\nAll remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑vertical and non‑sunny; the only such lines are horizontal (a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is forbidden). A horizontal line has a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate, so it can meet the anti‑diagonal \\(D\\) in **at most one** point. Therefore each of the \\(V-1\\) points of \\(D\\setminus\\{P_1,P_2\\}\\) would require a distinct horizontal line.\n\nConsequently the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{the sunny lines})\\;+\\;(V-1)\\;(\\text{horizontal lines})\\;=\\;V+1\n\\]\n\nnon‑vertical lines. But we have only \\(N=n-V\\) non‑vertical lines, and by (3) \\(V\\le n/2\\), whence\n\n\\[\nN=n-V\\le n-\\frac n2=\\frac n2\\le V .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(N\\le V\\), contradicting the necessity of at least \\(V+1\\) non‑vertical lines. The contradiction shows that a configuration with **two or more** sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\), and we have proved that no configuration can contain \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines. Hence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
