diagonal that is not already hit, which forces the total number of lines to be **strictly larger than \\(n\\)** when \\(s\\ge3\\).  \n\n   A careful counting of the required horizontal lines shows that for any \\(n\\ge3\\) the presence of three sunny lines forces at least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with three or more sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   Combining the constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) with the impossibility of \\(k\\ge3\\) we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the *\\(a\\)-th column*\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n\\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a ,\\qquad |S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a\\qquad(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\n(all non‑sunny) and add a sunny line that passes through the unique point of the last column, namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nOne convenient choice is  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-(2n-1),\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S\\) is sunny and contains \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThe vertical lines cover every point of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\); the sunny line covers \\((n,1)\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct and exactly one of them is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations with \\(k=2\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a\\qquad(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-2)\n\\]\n\n(all non‑sunny).  \nThe two remaining columns are  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad\nC_{n}   =\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine two distinct sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(n,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir equations are  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}x+\\frac{n-1}{\\,n-2\\,},\n\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=\\frac{2}{\\,n-1\\,}x .\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the two lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\n* \\(L_1\\) contains the point \\((n-1,2)\\);  \n* \\(L_2\\) contains the point \\((n,1)\\);  \n* the point \\((n-1,1)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\).  \n\nAll points of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-2}\\) are covered by the vertical lines, and the three points of the last two columns are covered as just described. Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly two of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of three or more sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(s\\ge3\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d+s=n .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  A vertical line is forced\n\nColumn \\(C_1\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  \nA sunny line is not vertical, therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(C_1\\).  \nConsequently the \\(s\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(s\\) points of \\(C_1\\); the remaining \\(n-s\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by non‑sunny lines.  \nThe only non‑sunny direction that can contain more than one point of a single column is the vertical direction; a horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line meets a column in at most one point.  \nHence at least one vertical line is needed, i.e.\n\n\\[\nv\\ge1 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  No column other than the first can contain a vertical line\n\nConsider any column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).  \nIts size is \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  \nIf a vertical line \\(x=a\\) were present, it would already cover the whole column \\(C_a\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) columns would then have to be covered by the other \\(n-1\\) lines.  \nBecause each of those lines can meet a given column in at most one point, the total number of points that can be covered in the remaining columns is at most \\((n-1)(n-1)\\).  \nSince  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=2}^{n}|C_b|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2<(n-1)^2\\qquad(n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nthe inequality does not lead to a contradiction; however, we can sharpen the argument by observing that **every** non‑vertical line already meets column \\(C_{v+1}\\) (the first column without a vertical line) in a distinct point (otherwise some point of that column would stay uncovered).  \nThus the set of non‑vertical lines is in bijection with the points of \\(C_{v+1}\\); consequently\n\n\\[\nn-v=|C_{v+1}|=n-v .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, the first \\(v\\) columns must all contain a vertical line, and there are **no** vertical lines among the columns \\(C_{v+1},\\dots ,C_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Two sunny lines already force an extra horizontal line\n\nLet the two sunny lines be \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \nBoth intersect the column \\(C_{v+1}\\) (the line \\(x=v+1\\)) at two distinct points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(v+1,y_1),\\qquad P_2=(v+1,y_2),\\qquad y_1\\neq y_2 .\n\\]\n\nThe two points lie on two different anti‑diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_1:\\;x+y=v+1+y_1,\\qquad D_2:\\;x+y=v+1+y_2 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(D_1\\) (resp. \\(D_2\\)) has the same sum of coordinates; a line that contains **two** distinct points of the same anti‑diagonal would have slope \\(-1\\) and therefore be non‑sunny.  \nHence each of the sunny lines meets its own anti‑diagonal only at the point where it crosses the column \\(x=v+1\\).\n\nAll remaining points of the two anti‑diagonals must be covered by non‑sunny, non‑vertical lines.  \nThe only such directions are horizontal lines (slope \\(0\\)).  \nA horizontal line can intersect a given anti‑diagonal in at most one point, so covering the two anti‑diagonals requires **at least two distinct horizontal lines**.\n\nThus we have shown that any configuration with \\(s\\ge2\\) sunny lines must contain at least\n\n\\[\nv\\ (\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;2\\ (\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;s\\ (\\text{sunny})\\ =\\ v+s+2\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Counting lines\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(v+h+d+s=n\\).  \nSince at least two horizontal lines are needed, \\(h\\ge2\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\nn=v+h+d+s\\ge v+2+s .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(s\\ge3\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn\\ge v+5 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, because the first \\(v\\) columns are already covered by the \\(v\\) vertical lines, the total number of points that still have to be covered equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=v+1}^{n}|C_a|\n   =\\frac{(n-v)(n-v+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of the remaining \\(n-v\\) non‑vertical lines can meet each of those columns in at most one point, so the maximal number of points they can cover is \\((n-v)(n-v)\\).  \nFor \\(v\\le n-3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-v)(n-v)<\\frac{(n-v)(n-v+1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  \nHence we must have \\(v\\ge n-2\\).  \nBut (7) together with \\(v\\ge n-2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn\\ge (n-2)+5=n+3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  \n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(s\\ge3\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\) and proved that no configuration can contain three or more sunny lines.  \nHence, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
