iagonal and therefore contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences; if there are \\(d\\) such lines the contribution is at most \\(d\\,n\\).*\n\n   *Summing all contributions we obtain*\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}\\,n\n   +\\underbrace{v+h+d}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\\,n\n   \\;\\ge\\; \\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|D_c|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Since the total number of lines is \\(n=v+h+d+k\\), the left‑hand side equals \\(n\\,n\\).  Hence\n   \\[\n   n^2\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(n=v+h+d+k\\) and cancelling the non‑sunny part gives exactly \\((★)\\).\n\n   Inequality \\((★)\\) forces \\(k\\le 1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Consequently **no configuration with two or more sunny lines can exist**.\n\n   (The derivation above is completely elementary and uses only the elementary facts that a line of any of the three forbidden directions meets each anti‑diagonal in exactly one point, while a sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal in at most one point.)\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of Sections 2 and 3 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   The inequality \\((★)\\) proved in Section 4 shows that \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.  \n\n   Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
