f \\(S_n\\).\n\n   The proof of Lemma 2 is a short elementary computation: solving the system\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   a+b=c,\\\\[1mm]\n   b-(n+1-i)=(i-n)(a-i)\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   gives the unique solution\n   \\[\n   a=c-i,\\qquad b=n+1-c,\n   \\]\n   which is a point of \\(S_n\\) for every admissible \\(c\\).\n\n3. **Construction for all \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   *Step A – the “full‑sunny” family.*  \n   The \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L^{\\;{\\rm all}}=\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_{n-1},\\;M\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where \\(M\\) is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) (its slope is \\(n-2\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\)), cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Indeed, each line \\(L_i\\) meets every anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) in the point \\((c-i,n+1-c)\\); the extra line \\(M\\) supplies the missing point \\((1,1)\\). Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\;{\\rm all}}\\). All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Step B – decreasing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) replace the first \\(t\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\) by the \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The remaining lines are the sunny lines \\(L_{t+1},\\dots ,L_{n-1},M\\).  \n   Because each anti‑diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) (with \\(c\\ge n-t+2\\)) is now present as a line, all its points are already covered; the still‑present sunny lines continue to meet every *remaining* anti‑diagonal (those with \\(c\\le n-t+1\\)) in a lattice point, exactly as in Lemma 2. Consequently the whole set \\(S_n\\) is still covered.\n\n   After the replacement we have\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{t}_{\\text{non‑sunny (anti‑diagonal)}}+\n   \\underbrace{(n-t)}_{\\text{sunny}}=n\n   \\]\n   lines, i.e. exactly \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since \\(t\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) to \\(n-2\\), we obtain configurations with every\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step C – the cases \\(k=0,1\\).*  \n   – \\(k=0\\): use the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \n   – \\(k=1\\): take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny) and one sunny line through the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n   Both families clearly cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Combining Steps A–C we have exhibited, for every \\(n\\ge 4\\), a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the four anti‑diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2=\\{(1,1)\\},\\;\n   D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\;\n   D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Existence.*  \n   – \\(k=0\\): the three anti‑diagonal lines \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\).  \n   – \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2\\) together with the sunny line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \n   – \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &\\ell_1:\\;y=x\\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n     &\\ell_2:\\;y-2=-2(x-1)\\quad\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\n     &\\ell_3:\\;y-2=-\\tfrac12(x-1)\\quad\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     which together cover all ten points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets the longest anti‑diagonal \\(D_4\\) in at most one point.  \n   With only two sunny lines at most two points of \\(D_4\\) can be covered, so at least one point of \\(D_4\\) must be covered by a non‑sunny line. A non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or an anti‑diagonal; any such line meets \\(D_4\\) in **exactly one** point (if it is vertical or horizontal) or in **all three** points (if it is the anti‑diagonal \\(D_4\\) itself).  \n\n   *Case 1.* The non‑sunny line is \\(D_4\\). Then the two sunny lines have to cover the remaining points \\((1,1