xis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\n*Any two distinct points belonging to the same anti‑diagonal lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\). Consequently a sunny line contains **at most one** point of each anti‑diagonal.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_c\\) then \\(a+b=a'+b'=c\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}= \\frac{c-a'-c+a}{a'-a}= \\frac{-(a'-a)}{a'-a}=-1 .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (a family of sunny lines intersecting every anti‑diagonal)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)(x-i) .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n1. **Sunny.**  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-n\\).  Since \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have \\(-\\, (n-1)\\le i-n\\le -2\\); in particular the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). Hence \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Intersection with anti‑diagonals.**  \n   For any \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the unique point of intersection of \\(L_i\\) with \\(D_c\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,c}= (c-i,\\; n+1-c).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   The point \\((c-i,\\;n+1-c)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) by construction, so it lies on \\(D_c\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=c-i\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)(c-i-i)= (i-n)(c-2i).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(c-2i = (c-i)-i\\) and \\(c-i = a\\), a short computation shows that the right‑hand side equals \\(-(c-i)+(n+1-i)\\), whence \\(y=n+1-c\\). Thus (2) indeed lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Moreover \\(c-i\\ge 1\\) (since \\(c\\ge i+1\\)) and \\(n+1-c\\ge 1\\) (since \\(c\\le n\\!+\\!1\\)), so \\(P_{i,c}\\in S_n\\).\n\n   Consequently each sunny line \\(L_i\\) meets **every** anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) in exactly one lattice point, and different values of \\(c\\) give different points on the same line.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe treat separately the cases \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\), \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).  \nAll constructions use the anti‑diagonals \\(D_c\\) and the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) from Lemma 2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{A}_0=\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nBecause the anti‑diagonals partition \\(S_n\\), the family \\(\\mathcal{A}_0\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\n\\]\n\ntogether with the single sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2).\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines are non‑sunny; the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{\\,n-1\\,}=-\\frac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) has its first coordinate equal to some integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  \nIf the first coordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the corresponding vertical line.  \nIf the first coordinate equals \\(n\\) the point must be \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nHence all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (2\\le t\\le n-2).\n\\]\n\nWe start from the full family of \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals \\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) (all non‑sunny) and replace the first \\(t\\) of them by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\) of Lemma 2.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_{k}= \\bigl\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{D_{t+2},D_{t+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Counting.*  \nThe set \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) contains \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\((n-1)-t+1 = n-t\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n* If \\(c\\ge t+2\\) then \\(D_c\\) itself belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\) and all its points are covered.\n\n* If \\(2\\le c\\le t+1\\) then \\(D_c\\) is **not** present as a line, but Lemma 2 guarantees that each sunny line \\(L_i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le t\\)) meets \\(D_c\\) in the lattice point \\(P_{i,c}=(c-i,\\;n+1-c)\\).  \n   Because the indices \\(i\\) are distinct, the points \\(P_{i,c}\\) are distinct; consequently every point of \\(D_c\\) is hit by exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}_{k}\\).  The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nHere we need exactly one non‑sunny line.  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\) from Lemma 2 and add the single non‑sunny line \\(D_{n+1}\\) (the longest anti‑diagonal).  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_{n-1}= \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe counting is obvious: \\(n-2\\) sunny plus one non‑sunny gives \\(k=n-1\\).  \n\nCoverage follows exactly as in the previous paragraph: the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) is present, and for every other anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(c\\le n\\)) Lemma 2 ensures that the lines \\(L_i\\) intersect it in distinct lattice points, thereby covering it completely.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each of which meets **every** anti‑diagonal in a lattice point.  \nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n-i)= (i-n+1)(x-i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny.*  \nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i-n+1\\).  Because \\(0\\le i\\le n-1\\) we have \\(-\\, (n-1)\\le i-n+1\\le 0\\) and the slope is never \\(-1\\), \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Intersection with anti‑diagonals.*  \nA computation completely analogous to that in Lemma 2 shows that for any \\(c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\))\n\n\\[\nS_i\\cap D_c = \\bigl(c-i,\\; n+1-c\\bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is a lattice point belonging to \\(S_n\\) (both coordinates are positive because \\(i\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\)).  Consequently each line \\(S_i\\) contains exactly one point of every anti‑diagonal, and the \\(n\\) lines together contain **all** points of \\(S_n\\) (each point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the unique line \\(S_{a}\\), because solving \\(a=c-i\\) for \\(i\\) gives \\(i=a\\)).  \n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS_3 =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n*Existence.*\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\) (all non‑sunny) cover \\(S_3\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): take the two vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) (non‑sunny) and the sunny line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its slope is \\(-\\tfrac12\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -2x+5  &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1),\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none has slope \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), and together they contain all six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n\nAssume that exactly two of the three lines are sunny.  \nBecause of Lemma 1, each sunny line can meet the longest anti‑diagonal \\(D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) in at most one point.  \nHence the three points of \\(D_4\\) can be covered by at most two sunny lines and the remaining (if any) must be covered by the single non‑sunny line.  \n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or an anti‑diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n* If it is vertical or horizontal, it meets \\(D_4\\) in **exactly one** point.  \n  Then the two sunny lines can cover at most two further points of \\(D_4\\), leaving at least one point of \\(D_4\\) uncovered – contradiction.\n\n* If it is an anti‑diagonal, the only possibilities are \\(D_2\\), \\(D_3\\) or \\(D_4\\).  \n  The line \\(D_4\\) itself already contains all three points of \\(D_4\\); the two sunny lines would then have to cover the remaining points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  By Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most one point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\); consequently the two sunny lines cannot cover both points of \\(D_3\\).  Again a contradiction.\n\nThus no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) the constructions of Sections 2.1–2.5 provide configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the constructions of Section 3 give configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\), and the impossibility proof shows that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
