f sunny lines intersecting every anti‑diagonal)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)(x-i) .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n1. **Sunny.**  The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-n\\).  Since \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) we have \\(-\\, (n-1)\\le i-n\\le -2\\); in particular the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). Hence \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n2. **Intersection with anti‑diagonals.**  \n   For any \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the unique point of intersection of \\(L_i\\) with \\(D_c\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,c}= (c-i,\\; n+1-c).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   The point \\((c-i,\\;n+1-c)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) by construction, so it lies on \\(D_c\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=c-i\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-(n+1-i)=(i-n)(c-i-i)= (i-n)(c-2i).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(c-2i = (c-i)-i\\) and \\(c-i = a\\), a short computation shows that the right‑hand side equals \\(-(c-i)+(n+1-i)\\), whence \\(y=n+1-c\\). Thus (2) indeed lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Moreover \\(c-i\\ge 1\\) (since \\(c\\ge i+1\\)) and \\(n+1-c\\ge 1\\) (since \\(c\\le n\\!+\\!1\\)), so \\(P_{i,c}\\in S_n\\).\n\n   Consequently each sunny line \\(L_i\\) meets **every** anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) in exactly one lattice point, and different values of \\(c\\) give different points on the same line.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for all admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe treat separately the cases \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\), \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).  \nAll constructions use the anti‑diagonals \\(D_c\\) and the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) from Lemma 2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{A}_0=\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑