e is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be sunny and let \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) with\n\\(a_2>a_1\\).  The slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\).\n\nBecause the slope is not \\(0\\), we have \\(b_2\\neq b_1\\); because it is not\n\\(-1\\), we cannot have \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|b_2-b_1|\\ge 1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad |b_2-b_1|\\neq a_2-a_1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)|\n   =|(a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)|\n   \\ge 2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the sums of the coordinates of two distinct points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\)\ndiffer by at least \\(2\\).  Since the possible sums range from \\(2\\) to\n\\(n+1\\), at most one point can be taken from each pair of consecutive\nsums; therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\),\nexactly \\(k\\) of them being sunny and the remaining \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny.\nA non‑sunny line can be taken to be an anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\); the\n\\(t\\) largest anti‑diagonals together contain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}(n-i)=\\frac{t\\,(2n-t+1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines\ncover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered; \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   +\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}\n   \\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (6) by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(2\\!\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor-k-1\\bigr)\\ge 0 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge 0\\), (7) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2\\!\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor-1=\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1\\;(n\\text{ even}),\\qquad\n0\\le k\\le n\\;(n\\text{ odd}).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (9).  Choose \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nd_1,d_2,\\dots ,d_k\\in\\{-(n-1),-(n-2),\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(d_i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + d_i .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the\n\\(L_i\\) are distinct because the intercepts are distinct.\n\nNow keep **all** anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  From this family\nremove any \\(k\\) of them (for definiteness, remove \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\))\nand replace them by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  The resulting\nfamily consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines: \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n\\(n-k\\) anti‑diagonals, i.e. precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n* If \\(d=d_i\\) for some \\(i\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line\n  \\(L_i\\) by definition (11).\n\n* If \\(d\\) is not among the chosen intercepts, then the point belongs to\n  the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\).  All anti‑diagonals are present in the\n  family (we removed only the first \\(k\\) of them, but \\(D_{a+b}\\) may be\n  one of the removed ones; however, if it were removed then its points\n  would have \\(d\\) equal to the corresponding intercept, contradicting\n  the assumption that \\(d\\) is not chosen).  Hence \\(D_{a+b}\\) is still\n  present and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n**Number of sunny lines.**  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny; all anti‑diagonals are\nnon‑sunny.  Hence the construction yields a configuration with precisely\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) satisfying (9).  When \\(n\\) is even,\n\\(k=n\\) is impossible because Lemma 1 gives the strict inequality  \n\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   = n\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nso the points cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines alone.  Hence the\nupper bound in (9) is also **sharp**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines\nis exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity (Lemma 1 and inequality (8)) and the sufficiency\n(construction above) have been proved rigorously."
