emma\u202f1, so they cover at most \\(kn\\) points.  All points of \\(S_n\\)\n   must be covered; using \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Simple algebra transforms (3) into  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\bigl(2\\!\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor-k-1\\bigr)\\ge0 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\ge0\\), (4) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 2\\!\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor-1=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a **necessary** condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-1\\;(n\\text{ even}),\\qquad\n   0\\le k\\le n\\;(n\\text{ odd}).                                   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency \u2013 explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  \n   Let \\(k\\le n-1\\).  Choose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines; for definiteness\n   take the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n\n   all of slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the anti\u2011diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n+2-(n-k)} .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has sum \\(s=a+b\\).  \n   If \\(s\\ge n+2-(n-k)=k+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the anti\u2011diagonal\n   \\(D_s\\), which is present.  \n   If \\(s\\le k+1\\) then \\(b-a\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\);\n   consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{b-a}\\).  \n   Thus all points are covered and exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2r+1\\).*  \n   Here the bound (5) allows \\(k=n\\).  We give a construction with **all**\n   \\(n\\) lines sunny.\n\n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-r\\le t\\le r\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = \\frac{t+1}{2}\\,x + \\frac{t+1}{2},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line of slope \\(\\dfrac{t+1}{2}\\) passing through the point\n   \\((1,\\,t+2)\\).  Because \\(t\\) runs t